      <span class="hero-eyebrow">Module 7 · Lesson 5</span>
      <h1>Transfer <span class="accent">Learning</span></h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Leverage pretrained models to solve new problems faster and with less data. Master fine-tuning strategies, domain adaptation, and how to adapt ImageNet pretrained models to specialized tasks like medical imaging and object detection.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="objectives">
      <h4>Learning Objectives</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Understand the principles and benefits of transfer learning</li>
        <li>Use pretrained models from Keras Applications (VGG, ResNet, Inception)</li>
        <li>Implement feature extraction: freeze pretrained weights and train only new layers</li>
        <li>Apply fine-tuning: selectively unfreeze and retrain deeper layers</li>
        <li>Handle domain shift through data augmentation and careful learning rates</li>
        <li>Build models for specialized domains (medical, remote sensing)</li>
        <li>Evaluate transfer learning effectiveness and compare with training from scratch</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section class="section" id="transfer-principles">
      <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
      <h2>Transfer Learning Principles and Benefits</h2>
      <p>Transfer learning leverages knowledge learned from large datasets (like ImageNet) to accelerate learning on new tasks with limited data. Pretrained models have already learned generic visual features: edges, textures, shapes. These features are valuable for almost any vision task. Rather than learning from random initialization, transfer learning starts with learned features and adapts them to new domains.</p>

      <p>Two main strategies exist: feature extraction (freeze pretrained weights, train only new layers) and fine-tuning (selectively unfreeze deeper layers and retrain). Feature extraction is faster and works well with small datasets. Fine-tuning takes longer but adapts learned features to the new task, often achieving better accuracy with sufficient data.</p>

      <div class="key-concept">
        <strong>Key Concept:</strong> Pretrained models trained on ImageNet for 1000 classes transfer remarkably well to unrelated tasks. The learned representations are general-purpose, making transfer learning one of the most powerful techniques in deep learning for practical applications.
      </div>

      <h3>Pretrained Models Available</h3>
      <p>Keras Applications provides many pretrained models: VGG (simple, effective), ResNet (skip connections, very deep), InceptionV3 (multi-scale convolutions), MobileNet (lightweight for mobile), and EfficientNet (optimal efficiency-accuracy trade-off). Choose based on your speed and accuracy requirements.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="va">tensorflow.keras.applications</span> <span class="kw">import</span> VGG16, ResNet50, MobileNetV2
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="va">tensorflow.keras.preprocessing</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">image</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Load pretrained models</span>
<span class="va">vgg</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">VGG16</span>(<span class="va">weights</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'imagenet'</span>)  <span class="cm"># includes top layers</span>
<span class="va">vgg_base</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">VGG16</span>(<span class="va">weights</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'imagenet'</span>, <span class="va">include_top</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">False</span>)  <span class="cm"># only conv base</span>

<span class="va">resnet</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">ResNet50</span>(<span class="va">weights</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'imagenet'</span>)
<span class="va">mobilenet</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">MobileNetV2</span>(<span class="va">weights</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'imagenet'</span>, <span class="va">input_shape</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="nm">224</span>, <span class="nm">224</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>))</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="feature-extraction">
      <span class="section-label">Practical Strategies</span>
      <h2>Feature Extraction and Fine-Tuning</h2>
      <p>Feature extraction freezes all pretrained weights and trains only new layers on top. This is fast (no backprop through the pretrained model) and works well when target task data is small. Fine-tuning unfreezes deeper layers gradually and retrains them with small learning rates, adapting learned features to the new task.</p>

      <h3>Feature Extraction Strategy</h3>
      <p>Use a pretrained base and add new layers for your task. Train only the new layers while keeping the base frozen. This prevents overfitting to small datasets while using powerful feature extraction from ImageNet training.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Feature extraction: freeze pretrained base</span>
<span class="va">base_model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">ResNet50</span>(<span class="va">weights</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'imagenet'</span>, <span class="va">include_top</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">False</span>, <span class="va">input_shape</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="nm">224</span>, <span class="nm">224</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>))

<span class="cm"># Freeze base model: don't train these weights</span>
<span class="va">base_model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">trainable</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">False</span>

<span class="cm"># Add new layers for your task</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">tf.keras.Sequential</span>([
    <span class="va">base_model</span>,
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">GlobalAveragePooling2D</span>(),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">256</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dropout</span>(<span class="nm">0.5</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'softmax'</span>)  <span class="cm"># 10 classes</span>
])

<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">compile</span>(<span class="va">optimizer</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'adam'</span>, <span class="va">loss</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'categorical_crossentropy'</span>, <span class="va">metrics</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'accuracy'</span>])

<span class="cm"># Train only the new layers (base frozen)</span>
<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">x_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>, <span class="va">epochs</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">batch_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">32</span>)</span></code></pre>

      <h3>Fine-Tuning Strategy</h3>
      <p>After feature extraction plateaus, unfreeze deeper layers and train with a small learning rate. This adapts learned features to your task while preserving knowledge from ImageNet. Start with frozen base, train new layers, then gradually unfreeze deeper blocks.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Fine-tuning: unfreeze and retrain deeper layers</span>
<span class="cm"># First: train with frozen base (as above)</span>

<span class="cm"># Then: unfreeze top blocks and fine-tune</span>
<span class="va">base_model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">trainable</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">True</span>

<span class="cm"># Freeze all but last 2 blocks</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">layer</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">base_model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">layers</span>[:<span class="op">-</span><span class="nm">2</span>]:
    <span class="va">layer</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">trainable</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">False</span>

<span class="cm"># Recompile with small learning rate</span>
<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">compile</span>(
    <span class="va">optimizer</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">optimizers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Adam</span>(<span class="va">learning_rate</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.0001</span>),  <span class="cm"># small lr</span>
    <span class="va">loss</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'categorical_crossentropy'</span>,
    <span class="va">metrics</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'accuracy'</span>]
)

<span class="cm"># Fine-tune for a few more epochs</span>
<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">x_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>, <span class="va">epochs</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="va">batch_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">32</span>)</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="domain-adaptation">
      <span class="section-label">Advanced Topics</span>
      <h2>Domain Adaptation and Specialized Applications</h2>
      <p>Domain shift occurs when source data (ImageNet) differs significantly from target data (medical imaging). Address this with careful data augmentation, batch normalization adjustments, and multi-stage fine-tuning. Specialized domains like medical imaging, satellite imagery, and face recognition all benefit from transfer learning adapted to domain-specific challenges.</p>

      <h3>Domain Adaptation Techniques</h3>
      <p>When transferring to different domains, data augmentation becomes critical. Normalize to the same range as ImageNet training. Consider adjusting batch normalization statistics. Use callbacks to monitor validation performance and prevent overfitting on small datasets.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Domain-adapted transfer learning</span>
<span class="cm"># Normalize to ImageNet statistics</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="va">tensorflow.keras.applications.resnet50</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">preprocess_input</span>

<span class="va">data_augmentation</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">tf.keras.Sequential</span>([
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">RandomFlip</span>(<span class="st">"horizontal"</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">RandomRotation</span>(<span class="nm">0.2</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">RandomZoom</span>(<span class="nm">0.2</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Rescaling</span>(<span class="nm">1.</span><span class="op">/</span><span class="nm">255</span>)
])

<span class="cm"># Build model with augmentation in pipeline</span>
<span class="va">base_model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">ResNet50</span>(<span class="va">weights</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'imagenet'</span>, <span class="va">include_top</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">False</span>)
<span class="va">base_model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">trainable</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">False</span>

<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">tf.keras.Sequential</span>([
    <span class="va">data_augmentation</span>,
    <span class="va">base_model</span>,
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">GlobalAveragePooling2D</span>(),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">256</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dropout</span>(<span class="nm">0.5</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">num_classes</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'softmax'</span>)
])</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="exercises">
      <span class="section-label">Practice</span>
      <h2>Exercises and Challenges</h2>

      <h3>Exercise 1: Cats vs Dogs Classification</h3>
      <p>Use MobileNetV2 for binary classification of cats vs dogs. Compare training from scratch vs transfer learning. Measure accuracy and training time for both approaches.</p>

      <h3>Exercise 2: Medical Imaging with Transfer Learning</h3>
      <p>Apply transfer learning to chest X-ray classification. Use ResNet50 pretrained on ImageNet. Implement feature extraction followed by fine-tuning. Handle class imbalance.</p>

      <h3>Exercise 3: Domain Adaptation</h3>
      <p>Train a model on CIFAR-10 (source), then adapt to CIFAR-100 or a custom dataset (target). Evaluate different fine-tuning strategies and learning rates.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="key-takeaways">
      <span class="section-label">Summary</span>
      <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>

      <div class="takeaway-grid">
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Transfer Benefits</h4>
          <p>Pretrained models provide learned features, enabling faster training and better generalization with limited data compared to training from scratch.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Feature Extraction</h4>
          <p>Freeze pretrained weights and train only new layers. Fast, efficient, effective for small datasets. Good baseline for transfer learning.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Fine-Tuning Strategy</h4>
          <p>Unfreeze deeper layers and retrain with small learning rates. Adapts learned features to new tasks. Works best with larger target datasets.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Pretrained Models</h4>
          <p>VGG (simple), ResNet (deep), MobileNet (fast), EfficientNet (optimal). Choose based on speed and accuracy requirements of your application.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Domain Adaptation</h4>
          <p>Data augmentation and careful normalization address domain shift. Monitor validation to prevent overfitting on small specialized datasets.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Learning Rates</h4>
          <p>Use standard rates for new layers. Use much smaller rates (10x-100x smaller) when fine-tuning pretrained weights to preserve learned features.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

